Serve the application and answer its data contract

Six routes now: the landing page at "/", the application at "/app", the asset
tree under "/assets/", the email gate at "/gate", the client contract at
"/api/state", and the existing write action left untouched for stage two.

The old page's staleness property is kept rather than traded away. It could never
be stale because it was rebuilt whole on every GET; the application inverts that
into a fixed shell plus /api/state, and /api/state still rebuilds from scratch
every time. The property simply moved to the half that carries the numbers, which
is the only half that could go stale.

/api/state does not check the token, and that is the existing policy rather than a
relaxation of it: GET / never checked it either, and the token exists so the page
can put it in its forms. What protects the read is the pair the server already
had -- the loopback bind and the Host check against DNS rebinding. A page on
another origin can send the request but, with no Access-Control-Allow-Origin in
the response, cannot read the answer. A test asserts that header stays absent,
because adding it would hand any page in the reader's browser his squad.

The gate accepts anything shaped like an address, sets a session cookie, and
STORES NOTHING -- not to a file and not to the log, since "we only wrote it to
stderr" is still having written it down. Accepting anything is deliberate: the
gate stands in for a signup flow that does not exist, and refusing would pretend
to a capability nothing behind it has. Storing nothing means there is no file of
personal data sitting in a working tree with no retention answer, decided by
default before anyone decided it was wanted.

capture.Replay is new and is the reason the tests below are real rather than
skipped. The archive held complete bootstraps that nothing could turn back into
one -- Store unmarshals into a player-status index and discards the rest -- so
every test wanting an engine either hit the network or asserted nothing. Replay
decodes the committed GW1 capture, and at GW1 the engine has no priors or recency
to fetch anyway, so an engine built from it is what production actually produces
rather than an approximation. The tests therefore Fatal rather than Skip on a
bad capture: it is committed, so an unreadable one is a broken repository.

The end-to-end tests run the real optimiser over those bytes and assert the
things a card cannot render without: every player has a name, a club, a position,
a price and a role band; the eleven and the bench are disjoint and together are
all fifteen; and nobody wears an armband from outside the eleven. The client is
forbidden from computing, so it has no way to notice any of those itself.
